      "summary": "The SQL implementation persists and retrieves traces and covers the listed behaviors, but the composite key can reject distinct identifiers on a case-insensitive database.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api/Persistence/Migrations/20261015120000_AddInvestigationTraces.cs",
          "line": 33,
          "message": "The composite key inherits the database collation, so case-insensitive collations collapse identifiers that the contract compares exactly. Use a case-sensitive key collation so differently cased identifiers remain distinct.",
          "confidence": 0.83,
          "category": "logic-error",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "The trace primary key uses SQL string columns without an explicit case-sensitive collation; on a case-insensitive database, the key treats differently cased identifiers as equal. AppendTrace then translates the resulting DbUpdateException into TraceConflictException.",
            "failureScenario": "On a case-insensitive database, after appending a trace with RunId \"run-1\", appending another for the same case and investigation with RunId \"RUN-1\" fails as a duplicate, even though the identifiers differ exactly. The SQL store therefore cannot persist both distinct traces allowed by the file-backed contract."
          }
        }
      ]
